      ":question/id": "841f3191-5c7e-5e47-b725-127033cb9a73",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-79453",
      ":question/problem": "If ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$"
    },
