      ":question/id": "9796dfce-fb6b-57bd-b442-e9278daaaa20",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-98925",
      ":question/problem": "If ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$"
    }
  ],
  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186054537,
    ":question/id": "5a566673-4529-57b8-87c9-d5a6118e0398",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4082",
    ":question/problem": "Express $\\log(3)+4\\log(y)$ as a single logarithm.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the second term to obtain\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log(3)+\\log(y^4).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+\\log(y^4)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$"
  },
